FINDINGS:
1. [MAJOR] src/formats.rs:1058 — `zip_read` decodes every entry name as UTF-8 and never consults general-purpose bit 11 (the UTF-8 flag it reads at 1045); a valid stored zip whose non-ASCII names were written without that flag (CP437, the zip default) is refused with "an entry name is not UTF-8", although SPEC.md says `zip.read` "reads one" and documents only compression/encryption refusals. Fix: honour bit 11 and decode CP437 when it is clear (or at minimum use `from_utf8_lossy` with a documented caveat). This was traced from the flag read at :1045 through the unconditional decode at :1058; a stored zip with a CP437-named entry would settle it and is currently absent.
2. [MINOR] src/formats.rs:1124 — `find_zip_end` accepts the last `ZIP_END` signature anywhere in the final 64 KiB without validating that its directory/comment reaches EOF, so a valid archive whose trailing comment contains `PK\x05\x06` is read as if that comment byte pattern were the EOCD and is misparsed or refused. Fix: accept a candidate only when `at + 22 + comment_len == bytes.len()`.
3. [MINOR] src/formats.rs:899 — `field_text` truncates a tar name at the first NUL, while `write_field` (:792) writes the entire name; a record key containing `\0` (reachable through `json.parse`) round-trips through `tar.write`/`tar.read` to a different name. `archive_entries` (:685) validates contents but not names. Fix: reject names containing NUL (and consider rejecting duplicates, which `archive_value` currently collapses) before writing either archive.
4. [MINOR] SURVEY.md:115 — the re-anchored module table cites `toml` at `src/stdlib.rs:202`, but the row is at :205; the same three-line drift affects `list` (SURVEY 147 vs actual 150), `network` (154 vs 157), `tar` (205 vs 208), `uuid` (203 vs 206) and `zip` (204 vs 207), all verified against the read `MODULE_MEMBERS`. The phase REPORT claims these citations were re-anchored, so an auditor following them lands on the wrong module. Fix: re-anchor the single-line rows.
5. [MINOR] tests/data_formats_test.rs:329 — `edge_archive_rejects_missing_and_malformed_files` is named for archives but only exercises `zip.read`; the `tar.read` missing-file and malformed-file paths (`src/runtime.rs:3787`, `src/formats.rs:815`) have no equivalent program-level test, so tar's error wiring could regress silently. Fix: loop the test over `["zip", "tar"]`.

